-- Lesson 1: What is a Variable?
'{"hook":"For centuries, mathematicians wrote problems in full sentences. One Persian scholar changed everything by turning words into symbols — and gave us the word algebra itself.","inventor":{"name":"Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi","birth_year":780,"death_year":850,"nationality":"Persian","bio":"A scholar at the House of Wisdom in Baghdad who wrote al-Kitab al-Mukhtasar fi Hisab al-Jabr wal-Muqabala (The Compendious Book on Calculation by Completion and Balancing) around 820 CE. The word algebra comes from al-jabr in the title."},"year_invented":820,"etymology":{"word":"algebra","from":"Arabic","parts":[{"root":"al-jabr","meaning":"completion or restoration"},{"root":"al-muqabala","meaning":"balancing"}]},"story_paragraphs":["In 9th-century Baghdad, the Abbasid Caliph al-Mamun founded the House of Wisdom — a grand library and research center that attracted scholars from across the Islamic world. Among them was al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician who would change how humans think about numbers.","Before al-Khwarizmi, solving equations meant writing out long sentences: ''A quantity, multiplied by itself, added to ten of the same quantity, equals thirty-nine.'' Al-Khwarizmi developed systematic methods to solve these problems, writing the first true algebra textbook.","His methods spread across the Islamic world, then into medieval Europe through Latin translations. The word algorithm also comes from the Latin version of his name, Algoritmi. Two fundamental concepts in mathematics — algebra and algorithms — trace back to one man.","Today, variables like x and y are so natural we forget they were invented. Every time you write x + 5 = 12, you are using a system that took humanity centuries to develop, starting with al-Khwarizmi''s revolution in Baghdad."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"Diophantus","contribution":"Greek mathematician (3rd century CE) who used abbreviations for unknowns, a precursor to variables"},{"name":"François Viète","contribution":"French mathematician who in 1591 introduced the use of letters for both known and unknown quantities"},{"name":"René Descartes","contribution":"Standardized using x, y, z for unknowns and a, b, c for constants in 1637"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"Computer Programming","icon":"💻","description":"Every programming language uses variables to store and manipulate data"},{"name":"Physics Formulas","icon":"🔬","description":"F = ma, E = mc² — variables represent real physical quantities"},{"name":"Spreadsheets","icon":"📊","description":"Cell references like A1, B2 are variables that hold values"},{"name":"Recipes & Scaling","icon":"🍳","description":"If a recipe serves 4 and you need to serve x people, variables help you scale"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"Portrait of al-Khwarizmi","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":null}}'::jsonb)
